## 3. Screen specs

### 3.1 This Week in Your Family — `1a`

- Masthead: "This Week in Your Family" / `JUL 6 – JUL 12, 2026` + "6 chosen from 83 anniversaries".
- **Featured entry** (one per week, not one per day): mono orange kicker `FEATURED · MONDAY, JULY 6`; name at 60px Playfair; relationship in serif italic 21px; mono lifespan + "died N years ago"; place on a hairline; narrative at 20px/1.62 with a 62px orange drop cap; actions "Their full story →" and "See her on the map".
- **"Also this week"** below a double rule: ledger rows of `DAY · DATE | name + event line | relationship (italic, right)`. Featured days carry a small `✦` in the mono date. Four to six rows, no cards.
- **Margin column:** imagery slot + caption; "The week's ledger" — 7 mono day rows with count bars (the highest bar orange); "While she lived" inset panel — three world events with the subject's age at each, linking to "Who else was alive →".

### 3.2 Explore — `2a`

Replaces four self-describing cards. Every section previews its own contents.

- **Search** as a broadsheet line, not an input box: mono `SEARCH` label + serif italic placeholder ("a name, a place, a year — 'Elizabeth Dane', 'Leyden', '1675'"), closed by a hairline. Focus turns it into a real field.
- **Where your family lived** — two-column ranked bar list of the top 8 places with counts; "All 613 places →".
- **Migration paths** — ledger rows: `England → Massachusetts` in Playfair 23, sub-line "peak c. 1679 · e.g. Jane Rossiter, arrived 1691", volume bar, mono count. Five rows + "All 34 paths →".
- **Kindred couples** — one row per couple: two names joined by an orange `∞`, mono lifespans, and on the right the kinship label in mono uppercase orange plus "via <shared ancestor>".
- **Margin column:** "Who was alive during…" — six eras as `YEAR | event | N of your people`; "People per century" histogram (18c leading, orange) with a one-sentence reading; "Loose threads" inset panel — 312 people with a year and no place, 9 orphan surnames, → "Turn one into a brief".

### 3.3 Map — `3a`

- Rail's contextual block becomes **Layers**: births & deaths (on), marriages, migration arrows, burial grounds.
- Masthead "The Map" / `613 PLACES · 4,182 EVENTS`.
- **Era band** as inline text tabs with mono counts (`All 4182 · 1600s 318 · 1700s 1190 …`), active underlined in orange — not a floating segmented pill over the map. Right side states the encoding: "Pin size = people recorded there".
- **Map (1fr, 560px tall) + 286px ledger**: "Busiest places, <era>", nine rows, selected row highlighted with an orange inset bar and synced to the map. Below it a "Reading the map" panel with one interpretive sentence.
- **Selected place spread** below a double rule: place name at 38px + mono `61 PEOPLE · 1660 – 1901 · 7 GENERATIONS`; a paragraph of interpretation; three stat columns (Earliest / Best known / Last recorded); actions. Margin: "Events here by century" histogram + a closing note.
- Pins/markers: sized by count, count printed inside. Never 300 equal pins — cap the rendered set and cluster the tail.

### 3.4 Nearby — `3b`

A different question from Map: *how close is my family's history to where I am right now?*

- Rail contextual block: "Your location — Hudson, New York" + "Change or set a trip →".
- Masthead "Nearby" / `FROM HUDSON, NEW YORK` + "21 towns · 4 within an hour's drive".
- **Headline is the answer:** "**109** of your family's people lived within **73 miles** of where you are" (40px Playfair, numbers in orange).
- **Radius control** with a real mono scale (5 / 25 / 50 / 73 / 100 mi) and the value in a mono chip — the control, not the map, is the primary instrument.
- Era band identical in behaviour to Map.
- **Two equal columns:** left, the 470px map (73-mile dashed orange ring, dark "you are here" dot) + a caption + a "Closest of all" panel naming the single nearest person with a human distance ("a twenty-minute drive down Route 9"). Right, **grouped by town, nearest first**: town name in Playfair 25 with a leader rule and mono `10.0 MI · 3 PEOPLE`, then person rows `name + italic relationship | MONO EVENT YEAR`, with "5 more in <town> →" when a group is long. Never repeat a town heading per person (the current app repeats "Claverack · 14.4 mi" four times).

### 3.5 Research (not yet designed — apply the same rules)

- **Render the markdown.** Briefs currently show `##` and `**` literally. Headings → Playfair; questions → a numbered ledger; sources → mono.
- Brief list: title in Playfair, mono `OPEN · 7/5/2026` status, ledger rows.
- On a brief page, demote the three big buttons: one orange primary ("Share this brief"), the two status changes become a small mono control group, not stacked full-width buttons.

### 3.6 Responsive

- **≥1200px:** rail + `1fr` + margin column, as specified.
- **900–1200px:** margin column collapses to the end of the main flow; rail stays.
- **<900px:** rail becomes the existing bottom tab bar, margin content becomes trailing sections, ledger rows stack to two lines. Type scale drops one step; the featured name stays at least 34px. This is where `1b`'s card patterns are acceptable.

