## 4. Prompt for Claude Code

> You are re-orienting the front end of **Witness Lives** (`app.witnesslives.com`), a React web app for exploring a family tree. The visual brand — warm paper background, high-contrast serif headlines, a single burnt-orange accent — is correct and must be preserved. The problem is layout architecture: the web app is the phone layout scaled up. Every object is an equal-weight rounded card in one centered column, so nothing is featured, entry-point screens show only their own titles, and desktop space is wasted.
>
> Read `Witness Lives — Web Redesign Handoff.md` (design system, structure rules, per-screen specs) and open `Web Treatment.dc.html` in a browser as the visual target. Option **1a** is the chosen direction; sections `2a`, `3a`, `3b` are the Explore, Map and Nearby targets. `map-canvas.html` is the working Leaflet map mock, including the pin-sizing formula and the tile-warming filter.
>
> **Scope, in order:**
> 1. **Shell.** Add a persistent 210px left rail for viewports ≥900px (wordmark, tree name, six destinations, a contextual slot per section). Keep the bottom tab bar below 900px. Introduce a page-shell component providing the masthead (title, right-aligned mono metadata, `3px double` closing rule) and the `1fr` + 286–320px margin grid.
> 2. **Design tokens.** Add the color, type, and spacing tokens from the handoff to the theme layer and replace ad-hoc values. Add the three font families (Playfair Display, Source Sans 3, IBM Plex Mono). Define a single `RecordText`/`.mono` treatment and use it for every date, year, count, and distance in the app.
> 3. **Kill the generic card.** Replace the shared card component in content areas with two primitives: `LedgerRow` (responsive column grid, hairline separator, hover tint) and `MarginPanel` (raised paper, 1px rule, small radius). Cards survive only under 900px.
> 4. **This Week.** One featured entry per week at full display scale with drop cap; all other days as ledger rows; the day-count ledger, imagery slot, and "While she lived" panel in the margin.
> 5. **Explore.** Delete the four navigation cards. Each section renders its own top-N data inline: ranked place bars, migration ledger with volume bars and peak decade, kindred couples with the `∞` spine and shared ancestor, plus the "Who was alive during…" list, century histogram, and "Loose threads" panel in the margin. All bars are CSS divs — no chart library.
> 6. **Map.** Convert the floating segmented era control into inline text tabs with counts. Put the map in the content column with a synced "Busiest places" ledger in the margin, and a selected-place spread beneath. Size pins by `22 + 26*sqrt(count/max)`, print the count in the pin, label only pins above 35% of max, cluster the tail, and apply the tile warming filter. Keep the existing tile provider and its attribution.
> 7. **Nearby.** Lead with the sentence answer and the radius control; group results by town (nearest first) with a mono `<distance> · <n> people` leader, and person rows beneath; never repeat a town heading. Include the "Closest of all" panel.
> 8. **Research.** Render brief markdown properly (headings, numbered research questions, emphasis) — no raw `##`/`**` anywhere. Reduce the three stacked full-width buttons to one primary plus a small status control group.
>
> **Constraints:** no new accent colors, gradients, or content-level drop shadows; no decorative illustration — the app must look finished with zero photographs; imagery slots must degrade to a striped placeholder with a mono caption; keep all existing routes, data fetching, and analytics intact — this is a presentation-layer refactor; ship each numbered step as its own reviewable commit, starting with the shell and tokens.
